1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,550
You have almost certainly used artificial intelligence today.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:06,548
A search suggestion.

3
00:00:06,398 --> 00:00:08,252
An autocorrected word.

4
00:00:08,102 --> 00:00:09,800
A recommended video.

5
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,807
But most people could not

6
00:00:11,657 --> 00:00:13,675
explain what it actually does.

7
00:00:13,975 --> 00:00:15,707
In the next few minutes,

8
00:00:15,557 --> 00:00:17,025
you will be able to.

9
00:00:17,575 --> 00:00:21,028
The chatbots and writing assistants you hear about are

10
00:00:20,878 --> 00:00:24,025
built on something called a large language model.

11
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:26,646
Its core job is easy to state.

12
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:27,804
Given some text,

13
00:00:27,654 --> 00:00:29,541
predict the text that is

14
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:31,350
most likely to come next.

15
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:34,833
Take the sentence: the capital of France is.

16
00:00:34,683 --> 00:00:36,970
The model assigns a probability

17
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:38,900
to every possible next word.

18
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,319
Paris gets a very high one.

19
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:43,725
Most other words get almost none.

20
00:00:44,025 --> 00:00:46,488
It picks one, adds it to the text,

21
00:00:46,338 --> 00:00:47,304
and repeats.

22
00:00:47,154 --> 00:00:48,597
One word at a time,

23
00:00:48,447 --> 00:00:51,250
that is how a full answer gets written.

24
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:52,710
More precisely,

25
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:55,337
it works in small pieces called tokens,

26
00:00:55,188 --> 00:00:56,954
which are often parts of

27
00:00:56,804 --> 00:00:58,975
words rather than whole words.

28
00:00:59,525 --> 00:01:02,276
So how does it know which word is likely?

29
00:01:02,126 --> 00:01:03,545
It was trained on an

30
00:01:03,395 --> 00:01:05,575
enormous amount of written text.

31
00:01:05,875 --> 00:01:06,803
In training,

32
00:01:06,653 --> 00:01:09,332
the model sees text with a word hidden,

33
00:01:09,182 --> 00:01:10,045
guesses it,

34
00:01:09,895 --> 00:01:12,250
and is corrected when it is wrong.

35
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:14,609
Repeated billions of times,

36
00:01:14,459 --> 00:01:18,852
this teaches it statistical patterns: grammar, common facts,

37
00:01:18,702 --> 00:01:20,125
styles of writing.

38
00:01:20,425 --> 00:01:22,167
Notice what this is not.

39
00:01:22,017 --> 00:01:23,824
It is not a database that

40
00:01:23,674 --> 00:01:25,482
looks up a stored answer.

41
00:01:25,332 --> 00:01:28,400
It generates new text from learned patterns.

42
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:31,900
Here is the part most people get wrong.

43
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,950
A model built to produce likely text

44
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,622
is also built to produce fluent text.

45
00:01:37,472 --> 00:01:40,150
And fluent is not the same as true.

46
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:41,951
Ask it for a source,

47
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:43,168
and it can write a

48
00:01:43,018 --> 00:01:45,465
citation with a believable author,

49
00:01:45,315 --> 00:01:48,100
title, and journal that does not exist.

50
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,733
This is called a hallucination.

51
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:52,353
The model is not lying.

52
00:01:52,203 --> 00:01:53,551
It has no intent.

53
00:01:53,401 --> 00:01:56,650
It is completing a pattern that looks right.

54
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:59,101
And its tone never changes.

55
00:01:58,951 --> 00:02:01,472
A wrong answer sounds exactly as

56
00:02:01,322 --> 00:02:03,325
confident as a right one.

57
00:02:03,875 --> 00:02:05,506
So where is it strong?

58
00:02:05,356 --> 00:02:08,535
Drafting, summarizing, rewriting, explaining,

59
00:02:08,385 --> 00:02:11,025
and organizing text that you give it.

60
00:02:11,325 --> 00:02:13,346
Where is it weak on its own?

61
00:02:13,196 --> 00:02:15,952
Exact facts, recent events, arithmetic,

62
00:02:15,802 --> 00:02:18,825
and any detail where a small error matters.

63
00:02:19,375 --> 00:02:21,652
That leads to one working rule.

64
00:02:21,502 --> 00:02:23,025
You stay the author.

65
00:02:23,325 --> 00:02:25,395
Use it to get a first version,

66
00:02:25,245 --> 00:02:27,699
then check every name, number, date,

67
00:02:27,549 --> 00:02:29,875
and quote before it goes anywhere.

68
00:02:30,175 --> 00:02:32,882
The more it matters if something is wrong,

69
00:02:32,732 --> 00:02:34,100
the more you verify.

70
00:02:34,650 --> 00:02:35,431
To recap.

71
00:02:35,281 --> 00:02:38,095
A language model predicts likely text.

72
00:02:37,945 --> 00:02:40,969
It learned patterns, not a list of facts.

73
00:02:40,819 --> 00:02:43,353
And it sounds equally sure whether

74
00:02:43,203 --> 00:02:44,825
it is right or wrong.

75
00:02:45,125 --> 00:02:47,002
Next lesson: how to ask,

76
00:02:46,852 --> 00:02:48,585
so the first draft you

77
00:02:48,435 --> 00:02:50,600
get back is actually useful.

78
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:52,857
Be the cog in the machine.

79
00:02:52,707 --> 00:02:54,525
Tools that work for you.
